          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"dumas-fils-la-dame-aux-camelias-49bccdd59b689488\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.96,\"analysis\":\"Armand presents his love for Marguerite as an established fact, reminding her of it to counter her fear that his father may persuade him to abandon her. The utterance is therefore strongly truth-conditional. Its reassuring function and the surrounding intention to remain together do not make the words themselves a relational undertaking, and there is no positive evidence of expressive or reflex-like discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The reassurance pragmatically suggests steadfastness, but it does not itself explicitly undertake or renew a commitment, so this does not warrant substantial P support.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Armand addresses Marguerite directly in represented dialogue: «Tu sais bien que je t'aime.»\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Marguerite fears that Armand's father will use her past to persuade Armand to leave her. Armand answers by invoking her existing knowledge of his love, chiefly as reassurance and as a reason she should discount that possibility. His surrounding refusal to abandon her and his plan to persuade his father strengthen the sincerity and constancy attributed to the avowal, while the later plot pressure does not alter its core force here.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"«Tu sais bien que je t'aime.»\",\"supports\":\"The affirmative present-tense clause presents Armand's loving state as true; «tu sais bien» frames it as already known rather than newly disclosed.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Marguerite says Armand's father may invent stories «pour que tu m'abandonnes» and that Armand may eventually be persuaded.\",\"supports\":\"The immediate conversational role is reassurance against Marguerite's fear of abandonment.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Armand has just proposed «Rester ensemble» and immediately afterward says that he, rather than his father, will do the persuading.\",\"supports\":\"The context portrays his avowal as sincere and steadfast, but locates the clearer future-directed resolve outside the core love utterance itself.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"No narration describes the words as involuntary, blurted out, or produced reflexively by overwhelming affect.\",\"supports\":\"There is no independent evidence for expressive/reflexive core force beyond ordinary emotionally engaged reassurance.\",\"confidence\":0.94}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the core avowal/reminder. Its reassuring conversational purpose and surrounding relationship conflict can be described contextually without adding P, E, or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":\"The supplied English translation was used only as an analytical aid; the classification is grounded in the French source passage.\"}"
        }
      ],
